{#- How It Works, rendered on both the homepage and the NaturalTag page.

    Two copies on screen, one copy in the CMS. Kirsty asked for it on the
    NaturalTag page as well as the homepage on 12 Aug, and Bryan chose to keep
    both. The obvious way to do that is to paste the section into the second
    template, and the obvious way is wrong: the four steps would then have two
    sets of words to keep in step, and the pair would drift the first time
    anyone edited one of them. This partial is the section; both pages include
    it; the words stay in home.yml and are edited once.

    Four steps across one row, with the detail below in a disclosure that is
    open by default.

    Alex Bond asked on 13 Aug for that detail to be fixed on the page rather
    than behind a click. `open` is the version of that request which keeps what
    the disclosure was for: the six lines are visible with no interaction, and
    anyone who wants the section back to one screen can still collapse it.
    Deleting the disclosure would have given the same first impression and
    taken the control away.

    <details> and <summary> rather than a hover or a scripted panel: they work
    with a tap, they work from a keyboard, a screen reader announces the open
    and closed state, and the text is in the page whether or not it is showing.
    A hover would fail all four.

    The id is the same on both pages, which is fine because they are separate
    documents, and it is what /naturaltag/#how-it-works and /#how-it-works both
    depend on. -#}
<section class="howitworks howitworks-compact" id="how-it-works">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="how-head">
      <span class="section-label">{{ home.how_label }}</span>
      <h2 class="section-title">{{ home.how_title }}</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="how-grid how-grid-compact">
      {% for step in home.how_steps %}
      <li class="how-step">
        <div class="how-step-num">{{ step.num }}</div>
        <h3>{{ step.title }}</h3>
        <p>{{ step.description }}</p>
      </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ol>
    {% if home.how_features %}
    <details class="how-more" open>
      <summary>{{ home.how_features_label }}</summary>
      <ul class="how-facts">
        {% for feature in home.how_features %}<li>{{ feature }}</li>{% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</section>
